{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 restricts 'objectively verifiable' outcomes to non-linear synthesis and declares the panel less reliable on that basis, but the problem never narrows verifiable outcomes to complex, non-linear tasks. For linearly verifiable tasks the panel following an explicit checklist can be more reliable than a single expert, so the blanket claim is unsupported by the problem as stated.","target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 treats adherence to 'negative-constraint' trap items as the evidence distinguishing reliability, but that only shows the panel resists intuitive pressure; it does not establish that the panel is more reliable than a single expert on average. A panel could pass trap items yet still err on aggregate verifiable outcomes, so the proposed evidence does not answer the problem's requested distinction.","target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 provides no claim or substantive content for assessment, so it fails to argue for or against the required claim about panel reliability versus a single expert and does not identify evidence distinguishing genuine reliability gain from checklist compliance, as the problem demands.","target_alias":"SRC_003"}]}